winewayland: stop demoting owned subsurfaces to the bottom of the sibling stack

Premiere's plugin panels sank behind the Program Monitor whenever they floated over it.

wayland_surface_reconfigure() positions an owned subsurface and then restacks it:

    if (toplevel_data->client_surface && <sibling check>)
        wl_subsurface_place_above(surface->wl_subsurface, client_surface->wl_surface);
    else if (!neutron_no_demote())
        wl_subsurface_place_above(surface->wl_subsurface, toplevel_surface->wl_surface);

The else branch was written as a safe fallback -- its comment says falling back to the
parent "is always valid". It is valid protocol and the wrong semantics:
wl_subsurface.place_above(parent) means DIRECTLY ABOVE THE PARENT'S OWN CONTENT, which is
the BOTTOM of the sibling stack, not the top.

Premiere's main frame has no client_surface of its own -- the client surfaces belong to
CHILD hwnds -- so the first branch never fired and the demote ran on every reposition of
the popup, dropping it beneath the Program Monitor's video subsurface.

Measured on the dev box while the popup was visibly sinking:

    zdiag  toplevel=0x10120 n=2 clients=1 | owned: found=1 nosub=0 badparent=0 added=1
    stats  5s calls=100 applies=1

neutron_restack_apply was doing its job -- it found the owned subsurface, its parent
matched the toplevel's current wl_surface, and it placed it on top. It applied ONCE; its
order cache then reported "unchanged" on every later call, so the per-reposition demote
won every time.

Doing nothing in that branch is both protocol-safe and correct: the subsurface keeps its
stacking and restack_apply owns the ordering. So NEUTRON_SUBSURFACE_NO_DEMOTE now defaults
ON; =0 restores the old demote for A/B.

Operator-confirmed: "no sink".

Also in here, found while measuring the above: wl_subsurface, toplevel_hwnd and
subsurface_parent are UNION members sharing storage with the toplevel role's xdg_toplevel,
xdg_toplevel_icon and decoration pointers. Both readers -- wayland_owned_subsurfaces_of()
and restack_apply()'s owned branch -- dereferenced them without checking the role, so
every toplevel appeared to have a wl_subsurface and its "toplevel_hwnd" was really an
xdg_toplevel_icon pointer. Verified live: hwnd 0x10120, role=TOPLEVEL, printed
subsurf=0x555594583ac0 top_hwnd=0x555594583b30. Toplevels were excluded only because that
garbage never happened to equal the HWND being searched for -- and place_above against a
non-sibling is a FATAL protocol error. Both now check the role first.

⚠️ This fix only reaches the panels when NEUTRON_OWNED_SUBSURFACE_CAPTIONED=1 and
NEUTRON_OWNED_ZORDER=1; both still default OFF.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
@@ -978,7 +978,18 @@
 static BOOL neutron_no_demote(void)
 {
     static int on = -1;
-    if (on < 0) { const char *e = getenv("NEUTRON_SUBSURFACE_NO_DEMOTE"); on = (e && e[0] == '1') ? 1 : 0; }
+    /* NEUTRON: DEFAULT ON since 2026-09-01. The guarded branch reads as a harmless fallback --
+     * "place above the parent, which is always valid" -- but wl_subsurface.place_above(parent)
+     * means DIRECTLY ABOVE THE PARENT'S OWN CONTENT, i.e. the BOTTOM of the sibling stack. For an
+     * owned window whose toplevel has no client_surface of its own (Premiere's main frame: the
+     * client surfaces belong to CHILD hwnds) the first branch never fires, so every reposition of
+     * the popup demoted it beneath the Program Monitor's video subsurface. neutron_restack_apply
+     * had already placed it correctly -- measured found=1 added=1 applies=1 -- and then its order
+     * cache reported "unchanged" forever, so the demotion always won. That is the panel-sinking
+     * bug. Doing nothing here is both protocol-safe and correct: a subsurface keeps its current
+     * stacking, and restack_apply owns the ordering. NEUTRON_SUBSURFACE_NO_DEMOTE=0 restores the
+     * old demote for A/B. */
+    if (on < 0) { const char *e = getenv("NEUTRON_SUBSURFACE_NO_DEMOTE"); on = (e && e[0] == '0') ? 0 : 1; }
     return on;
 }
 
@@ -2465,7 +2476,11 @@
         for (k = 0; k < no && n < NEUTRON_ZMAX; k++)
         {
             struct wayland_win_data *od = wayland_win_data_get_nolock(owned[k]);
-            if (!od || !od->wayland_surface || !od->wayland_surface->wl_subsurface) continue;
+            /* union safety, as in wayland_owned_subsurfaces_of(): these fields alias the
+             * toplevel role's pointers when the role is not SUBSURFACE. */
+            if (!od || !od->wayland_surface ||
+                od->wayland_surface->role != WAYLAND_SURFACE_ROLE_SUBSURFACE ||
+                !od->wayland_surface->wl_subsurface) continue;
             /* NEUTRON sibling-validity, matching the client-surface loop above. Selection came
              * from toplevel_hwnd, an HWND -- which stays equal across a destroy/recreate of the
              * toplevel's wl_surface (defocus, docking, any role change). The subsurface is then
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
@@ -144,7 +144,16 @@
     {
         if (n >= max) break;
         if (d->hwnd == toplevel) continue;
-        if (!d->wayland_surface || !d->wayland_surface->wl_subsurface) continue;
+        if (!d->wayland_surface) continue;
+        /* NEUTRON: wl_subsurface / toplevel_hwnd / subsurface_parent are UNION members shared with
+         * the toplevel role's xdg_toplevel / xdg_toplevel_icon / decoration pointers. Read on a
+         * non-subsurface they return those pointers reinterpreted -- so every toplevel looks like
+         * it has a wl_subsurface, and its "toplevel_hwnd" is really an xdg_toplevel_icon pointer.
+         * Toplevels were excluded only because that garbage never happened to equal the HWND we
+         * were looking for. Verified live 2026-09-01: hwnd 0x10120, role=TOPLEVEL, printed
+         * subsurf=0x555594583ac0 top_hwnd=0x555594583b30. Check the role before touching them. */
+        if (d->wayland_surface->role != WAYLAND_SURFACE_ROLE_SUBSURFACE) continue;
+        if (!d->wayland_surface->wl_subsurface) continue;
         if (d->wayland_surface->toplevel_hwnd != toplevel) continue;
         out[n++] = d->hwnd;
     }
